  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nA cold front is moving through northern KS very early this morning, \nshifting winds to the north-northeast. The front should be near I-70 \nby 09Z and completely through the CWA by 13Z. The air mass \nimmediately behind the front is slightly cooler and drier. \nTemperatures today will be about ten degrees cooler in northern KS \ncompared to yesterday, but only about two to five degrees cooler \nnear and south of I-70. The more significant difference will likely \nbe the lower dew points as the boundary layer mixes drier air down \nthis afternoon. Some CAMs, like the HRRR and RAP, are more aggressive\nwith mixing, which results in higher ambient temps. However, the \nofficial forecast sides more with HREF values. Given the slightly \nlower T/Td forecast, we were able to cancel the advisory for Coffey \nand Anderson Counties for today. While advisory criteria is not \nexpected, heat index values could still approach 100 degrees in those\nlocations. A few CAMs also show isolated showers moving out of \nsouthern Nebraska mid-morning. Have kept POPs less than 10 percent \nfor now.\n\nNoticeably cooler air arrives area-wide Wednesday as a surface ridge \nbecomes more established over the area. Highs will reach the mid to \nupper 80s with afternoon dew points mainly in the upper 50s. \nPrecipitation chances also return to the forecast on Wednesday, \nmainly for north-central and far northern KS initially. More \nwidespread POPs increase Wednesday night and Thursday morning on the \nnose of a LLJ and in a zone of isentropic ascent ahead of an \napproach mid-level shortwave trough. \n\nAnother round of rain is then likely (70-80% chance) Thursday night \nand early Friday when the aforementioned mid-level wave moves over \nthe area. Another LLJ will bring deeper moisture into northeast KS. \nModels seem to agree on pwats near 2.0 inches with at least minor \namounts of elevated instability. This is leading to concerns for \nheavy rainfall during this timeframe. NBM has a 60 percent chance \nfor at least one inch of rain at KTOP and about a 35 percent chance \nfor at least two inches. Excessive rainfall could lead to isolated\nflooding.\n\nDry weather then return to the forecast this weekend when an upper \nridge build back in from the southwestern US. Heat returns as well \nwith temperatures back to the 90s on Sunday. Heat index values could \nagain approach advisory criteria at that time.",
